Hi,

thank you...the wrapper around my equation solved the problem.

Now I have two working solutions but I am not really aware of its differences? What are the advantages what are the disadvantages of each approach?

In my case the I have to draw plots with always three functions/lines (for each line one set of sigma_stat,sigma_mob and p)...

Again here the working code so that everyone can try:

eq <- function(x,sigma_stat,sigma_mob,p) {
	(dnorm(x, sd = sigma_stat)*p)+(dnorm(x, sd = sigma_mob)*(1-p))
}

eq_tmp <- function(x) {
	eq(x,sigma_stat,sigma_mob,p)
}

sigma_stat <- 10
sigma_mob <- 100
p <- 0.3

x <- seq(-1000, 1000, .01)
tmp <- data.frame(x=x, y=eq(x,sigma_stat,sigma_mob,p))

# First approach

plot <- ggplot(tmp, aes(x=x, y=y))

plot + stat_function(fun=eq_tmp)

# Second approach

plot <- ggplot(tmp, aes(x=x, y=y))
plot + geom_line()

best regards,

